---
import Article from '../../layouts/Article.astro'
import AssertionCard from '../../components/AssertionCard.astro'
import ChartFigure from '../../components/ChartFigure.astro'
import DataTable from '../../components/DataTable.astro'
import { assertion } from '../../lib/feeds'
import { bar } from '../../lib/chart-spec'

const colonel = assertion('a-colonel-of-that-name-in-the-war-of-1812')

const colonelSpec = bar(
  colonel.figures.map((f) => f.label),
  colonel.figures.map((f) => f.value),
  { yTitle: 'The fourteen counties of 1820, by what Howe says about the name', labelValues: true },
)

// What Howe's 1847 formation sentence gives for each of the fourteen. Typed out because the
// attributions are quotations and the graph carries them as one measure.
const namesOf1820 = [
  ['Hancock', 'John Hancock, first president of the revolutionary congress'],
  ['Henry', 'Patrick Henry, the celebrated Virginian orator in the revolutionary era'],
  ['Marion', 'Gen. Francis Marion, of South Carolina, a partisan officer of the revolution'],
  ['Mercer', 'Gen. Hugh Mercer, a Virginia officer who fell at Princeton, Jan. 3d, 1777'],
  ['Paulding', 'John Paulding, of Peekskill, one of the three militia men who captured Major Andre'],
  ['Putnam', 'Gen. Israel Putnam, born at Salem, Mass., 1718, died at Brooklyn, Conn., 1790'],
  ['Van Wert', 'Isaac Van Wart, one of the three captors of Maj. Andre, died 1828, aged 68'],
  ['Williams', 'David Williams, of Tarrytown, one of the three captors of Maj. Andre'],
  ['Seneca', 'the tribe who had a reservation within its limits'],
  ['Allen', '“a colonel of that name in the war of 1812”'],
  ['Wood', '“the brave and chivalrous Col. Wood, a distinguished officer of engineers in the war of 1812”'],
  ['Crawford', '— no namesake given'],
  ['Hardin', '— no namesake given'],
  ['Sandusky', '— no namesake given'],
]
---

<Article slug="who-the-county-is-named-for">
  <AssertionCard assertion={colonel} />
  <p>
    Two men are usually offered: Ethan Allen of Vermont, and John Allen, a Kentucky colonel
    killed at the River Raisin in January 1813. The earliest general account of Ohio's counties
    this site holds is Henry Howe's, published in 1847 — thirty-eight years before the county's
    own first history — and it settles half the question in one clause: Allen was
    <em>"named in honor of a colonel of that name in the war of 1812."</em> Ethan Allen died in
    1789.
  </p>
  <p>
    What Howe has is a rank and a war, not a man, and forty years of revision did not supply one.
    Of the fourteen counties the act of 12 February 1820 laid out of the cession, he attributes
    eleven; eight of the persons he names in full, and two he gives only a rank and a war — Allen
    and Wood, the two most recent deaths on the list.
  </p>
  <ChartFigure
    id="counties-of-1820"
    title="The fourteen counties of 1820, by what Howe's formation sentence gives"
    caption="Every man Howe names in full belongs to the Revolution — including Paulding, Van Wert and Williams, the three militiamen who took Major André in 1780, given a county each on adjoining ground. The two he identifies only by rank and war are the two officers of 1812."
    columns={['', 'Counties']}
    rows={colonel.figures.map((f) => [f.label, f.value])}
    height={260}
  />
  <script type="application/json" data-chart-spec="counties-of-1820" set:html={JSON.stringify(colonelSpec)} is:inline />
  <DataTable
    title="What Howe's 1847 formation sentence says of each"
    caption="Read entry by entry rather than by search: the attribution appears only in the formation sentence, and a keyword search for “named in honor” finds two of the eleven."
    columns={['County', 'Named from']}
    rows={namesOf1820}
  />
  <p>
    The Ethan Allen story has a source and a date. The 1921 county history dates the act, then
    reasons from the company the name keeps — the assembly "evidently had in mind a number of
    Revolutionary patriots" — and turns to "the life and character of the patriot <em>seemingly</em>
    honored in the name of Allen County," followed by a page and a half on Ethan Allen. Both
    hedges are the book's own. Sixty-five pages earlier the same volume has Col. John Allen riding
    out with 110 men toward Frenchtown, and never joins the two.
  </p>
  <p>
    One correction falls out of this. This site used to say the 1885 county history "gives
    namesakes freely" and declines to give one here. It does not: its chapter on Ohio's counties
    runs to seventy-nine entries and carries exactly one namesake, its first. The silence about
    Allen was the book's habit, not a judgment about Allen.
  </p>
</Article>
